-- THE PERMUTATION ROW, ORDERING 2 of 2 (packet Amendment A condition 2): ONE
-- import spelling (the ALIAS spelling), ALL orderings of the re-export
-- declarations, asserting ONE SIGNATURE. Section 4 of this gate permutes `impl`
-- blocks and auto-derives its pair set from single-FILE fixtures, so it cannot
-- reach a multi-file re-export hub -- this explicit `-ab`/`-ba` pair is how
-- order-invariance-within-a-spelling stays graded for this shape. Two
-- declarations means 2! = 2 orderings, so the pair IS all of them.
--
-- WHAT VARIES ACROSS THE PAIR: only the ORDER of `lib/hub.mdk`'s two
-- `export import` lines. Definers, spellings, arithmetic and call shape are
-- byte-identical between the two fixtures.
--
-- WHY THIS SHAPE CAN CATCH SOMETHING THE OTHER ROWS CANNOT: the fix widened the
-- alias arm's "already mangled?" test from the ONE module the importer spelled to
-- the LOADED MODULE-ID SET. A SET-membership test must not care which member
-- matched, or in what order the set was built. Here the hub's public schemes
-- carry keys mangled for TWO different definers (`lib_pa__ga`, `lib_pb__gb`), so
-- an order-sensitive widening -- a first-match that could land on the wrong
-- module, or a supply list whose contents depend on fold order -- would make the
-- two orderings disagree on the value, the signature, or both.
--
-- GROUND TRUTH, HAND-DERIVED FROM THE DECLARATIONS BEFORE ANY BINARY WAS RUN --
-- not captured from the engine:
--   `ga : Int -> Int`, `ga x = x + 1` (lib/pa.mdk).
--   `gb : Int -> Int`, `gb x = x * 10` (lib/pb.mdk).
--   Both reach here through lib.hub's `export import`, which re-exports BY
--   IDENTITY, so `H.ga`/`H.gb` denote those same two bindings.
--   `combine x = H.ga x + H.gb x` is therefore `Int -> Int` -- the ONE signature
--   both orderings must produce (section 2 asserts exactly this line).
--   `combine 3` = (3 + 1) + (3 * 10) = 4 + 30 = 34.
--
-- ⚠️ THE TWO DEFINERS' ARITHMETIC DIFFERS ON PURPOSE (`+ 1` vs `* 10`). Had both
-- used `+ 1`, swapping which definer a key resolved to would leave the value
-- UNCHANGED and this pair would be inert -- it would pass on a compiler that had
-- confused the two bindings entirely.
import lib.hub as H

combine : Int -> Int
combine x = H.ga x + H.gb x

main = println (combine 3)
